pan class=\"mord mathrm\">True</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-0.01em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">True</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:3.35em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"arraycolsep\" style=\"width:0.5em;\"></span><span class=\"vertical-separator\" style=\"height:7.2em;border-right-width:0.04em;border-right-style:solid;margin:0 -0.02em;vertical-align:-3.35em;\"></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-2.5em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:5.85em;\"></span><span class=\"hline\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.7em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:5.85em;\"></span><span class=\"hline\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-4.9em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:5.85em;\"></span><span class=\"hline\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-6.1em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:5.85em;\"></span><span class=\"hline\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-7.3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:5.85em;\"></span><span class=\"hline\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-8.5em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:5.85em;\"></span><span class=\"hline\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-9.7em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:5.85em;\"></span><span class=\"hline\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:3.35em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></div></div></div>",
       "mime": "x-application/wolfram-language-html"
      },
      {
       "data": "Out[31]= \\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline\\text{sites} & \\text{dim} & \\text{\\#IoM from $\\tau$1} & \\text{from $\\tau$1,$\\tau$2} & \\text{from $\\tau$1,$\\tau$2,$\\tau$3} & \\text{+ $\\tau$4 (check)} & \\text{2 seeds agree}\\\\\\hline\\text{(1,1,1) twisted} & 216 & 3 & 9 & 12 & 12 & \\mathrm{True}\\\\\\hline\\text{(2,1,1) twisted} & 720 & 3 & 9 & 12 & 12 & \\mathrm{True}\\\\\\hline\\text{(1,1,1) z=1} & 216 & 2 & 6 & 6 & 6 & \\mathrm{True}\\\\\\hline\\text{(2,1,1) z=1} & 720 & 2 & 6 & 6 & 6 & \\mathrm{True}\\\\\\hline\\text{(2,2,1) z=1} & 2400 & 2 & 6 & 6 & 6 & \\mathrm{True}\\\\\\hline\\end{array}",
       "mime": "text/plain"
      }
     ],
     "id": "3b7ca8d2-2022-4d7d-8583-30702e2badd6"
    }
   ],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## Results (2026-09-11, exact)\n\nSanity (cell 4): at `L = 3`, `[1,1,0,0]^3` (dim 216), the `τ_a` commute and `τ_4 ∝ 1`, twisted and untwisted. Every count below agrees for two independent random vectors, and adding `τ_4` never changes it.\n\n| sites | dim | from `τ_1` | `+ τ_2` | `+ τ_3` = total |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| L=2 (1,1), (2,2) twisted | 36, 400 | 2 | 6 | **8** |\n| L=2 (2,2), (3,3) z=1 | 400, 2500 | 1 | 2 | **2** |\n| L=3 (1,1,1), (2,1,1) twisted | 216, 720 | 3 | 9 | **12** |\n| L=3 (1,1,1), (2,1,1), (2,2,1) z=1 | 216, 720, 2400 | 2 | 6 | **6** |\n\n**L = 2** reproduces the orthogonality notebook: 8 twisted, 2 untwisted.\n\n**L = 3: 12 twisted, 6 untwisted**, stable as the spins grow.\n\n- **Twisted count.** 12 = 3 weights + 9 free coefficients. `τ_1` (degree 3) has `u^2` (weights), `u^1` and `u^0`. `τ_2` (degree 6) has `u^5` (weights) and `u^4 .. u^0`. `τ_3` is a fixed degree-6 factor times a cubic, with `u^2` (weights), `u^1` and `u^0`. The counts per transfer matrix, 3 + 6 + 3, match this exactly.\n- **Zero twist.** `τ_1` keeps 2, both non-weight coefficients. `τ_2` adds only 4 of its 5. `τ_3` adds nothing. So 6 are lost: the 3 weights, 1 combination in `τ_2`, and both free coefficients of `τ_3`.\n- **Observation (not derived).** The number lost is 6 at both `L = 2` (8 → 2) and `L = 3` (12 → 6), the number of positive roots of gl(4). Testing `L = 4` would distinguish this from coincidence.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## L = 4: is the number lost at zero twist always 6?\n\nPrediction from the coefficient structure: twisted `= 3` weights `+ 13` free coefficients `= 16`. Here `τ_1` (degree 4) has 3 free coefficients, `τ_2` (degree 8) has 7, and `τ_3` (a fixed degree-8 factor times a quartic) has 3. If 6 are lost again, the untwisted count is **10**. Fourth inhomogeneity `θ_4 = 3/11`.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "(* ---- 7. L = 4 ---- *)\nres4 = With[{zt = {2, 3, 1/2, 1/5}, z1 = {1, 1, 1, 1}, s4 = Function[{a, b, c, d}, {{{a, a, 0, 0}, 1/3}, {{b, b, 0, 0}, 1/7}, {{c, c, 0, 0}, 2/5}, {{d, d, 0, 0}, 3/11}}]},\n   {countRow[\"(1,1,1,1) twisted\", s4[1, 1, 1, 1], zt], countRow[\"(1,1,1,1) z=1\", s4[1, 1, 1, 1], z1], countRow[\"(2,1,1,1) z=1\", s4[2, 1, 1, 1], z1]}];\nGrid[Prepend[res4, countHeader], Frame -> All]",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
   "outputs": [
    {
     "items": [
      {
